==List of Corrections==

'''Since the end of summer 5780 (2020), all corrections and improvements in our edition are listed in an orderly fashion on the '{{u|changes}}' page in the [https://docs.google.com/spreadsheets/d/1mkQyj6by1AtBUabpbaxaZq9Z2X3pX8ZpwG91ZCSOEYs/edit?usp=sharing spreadsheet].'''<ref>Beginning in the summer of 5780 (2020), a careful comparison was made between the [https://he.wikisource.org/wiki/%D7%9E%D7%A9%D7%AA%D7%9E%D7%A9:Dovi/%D7%9E%D7%A7%D7%A8%D7%90_%D7%A2%D7%9C_%D7%A4%D7%99_%D7%94%D7%9E%D7%A1%D7%95%D7%A8%D7%94 Miqra according to the Masorah] and the [https://www.hcanat.us/Tanach.xml latest UXLC edition], as a part of preparing to upload the biblical text to the [https://www.sefaria.org.il/new-home Sefaria] website.  The goal of the comparison was first of all to discover whether there were major differences between the two editions (amounting to verses or whole words).  After that the purpose of the comparison was details: hundreds of changes in matters of spelling and special letters, and more than a thousand changes in points and accents were compared and examined. This comparison has led to tens of corrections and improvements in our addition (as well as in the [https://www.hcanat.us/Tanach.xml UXLC] edition).</ref>

It is possible to track changes made earlier in the "''past revisions''" tab of each chapter in the Bible.<ref>In the case of the Torah, it is possible to track the older changes in the "past revisions" tab of the [https://he.wikisource.org/wiki/%D7%9E%D7%A9%D7%AA%D7%9E%D7%A9:Dovi/%D7%AA%D7%95%D7%A8%D7%94_%D7%A2%D7%9C_%D7%A4%D7%99_%D7%94%D7%9E%D7%A1%D7%95%D7%A8%D7%94 original edition], which was divided according to the weekly Torah portions. For complete information on the previous versions of the project (which can be found in "past revisions") see [https://he.wikisource.org/wiki/%D7%9E%D7%A9%D7%AA%D7%9E%D7%A9:Dovi/%D7%9E%D7%A7%D7%A8%D7%90_%D7%A2%D7%9C_%D7%A4%D7%99_%D7%94%D7%9E%D7%A1%D7%95%D7%A8%D7%94/%D7%9E%D7%99%D7%93%D7%A2_%D7%A2%D7%9C_%D7%9E%D7%94%D7%93%D7%95%D7%A8%D7%94_%D7%96%D7%95/%D7%A0%D7%A1%D7%A4%D7%97%D7%99%D7%9D#%D7%A9%D7%9C%D7%91%D7%99_%D7%94%D7%A2%D7%91%D7%95%D7%93%D7%94_%D7%91%D7%A4%D7%99%D7%AA%D7%95%D7%97_%D7%94%D7%A4%D7%A8%D7%95%D7%99%D7%A7%D7%98 below].</ref> For that purpose one can use "version history" generally, and "edit history" for each cell in the [https://docs.google.com/spreadsheets/d/1mkQyj6by1AtBUabpbaxaZq9Z2X3pX8ZpwG91ZCSOEYs/edit?usp=sharing spreadsheet].

==List of Sources (Manuscripts and Printed Books) and Tools==

===The Two Main Manuscripts which Underlie the Biblical Text===

'''The text of our edition is based upon two primary manuscripts: the Aleppo Codex and the Leningrad Codex.'''

'''A. The Aleppo Codex''' (Hebrew כתר ארם צובה, also כתי"א or הכתר or א or A)
* '''Photographs of the manuscript''': There are several places where photographs of the Aleppo Codex are available for free download. The most complete and high-quality file is found [https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1_Aleppo_Codex_FULL_high-resolution.pdf here on Wikimedia Commons], and for easy reading and browsing of the same scan, see its source here at the [https://archive.org/details/Aleppo_Codex Internet Archive] (all existing pages in this scan are of high quality). Additional files of the Aleppo Codex can be found on [https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Category:Aleppo_Codex Wikimedia Commons] and the Internet Archive. The Internet Archive has another direct scan of the manuscript [https://archive.org/details/Aleppo_Codex_direct_scan here], and other downloads [https://archive.org/details/Aleppo_Codex_direct_scan here] and [https://archive.org/details/aleppo-fascimile-full here].
* '''Navigating the manuscript by chapters''': to navigate the manuscript by selected chapter, see ''Mikra'ot Gedolot 'Haketer''' [https://www.mgketer.org/ website], and in the menu on the left choose עיון במאגר ‘הכתר’‬ ('Browse the Aleppo Codex Database'), then select הצג > תנ”ך ועזרים נוספים > צילום כת”י כתר ארם צובה, that is, View > Bible and Additional Helps > Photocopy of the Aleppo Codex.
* ‮א(ס)‬ or A(S) = the text of the Aleppo Codex according to Yaakov '''S'''apir according to ‮מאורות נתן‬ (''Meorot Natan'')‬ – see [https://daat.ac.il/daat/vl/tohen.asp?id=278 here] and [http://www.daat.ac.il/daat/vl/meorotnatan/meorotnatan01.pdf here].
* ‮א(ק)‬ or A(K) = the text of the Aleppo Codex according to the testimony of Yehoshua Kimchi as related by Yosef Ofer’s article ‮כתר ארם צובא והתנ”ך של ר’ שלום שכנא ילין‬ (“The Aleppo Codex and the Tanakh of Rabbi Shalom Shachna Yellin” – [https://daat.ac.il/daat/vl/tohen.asp?id=277 link], [http://www.daat.ac.il/daat/vl/yosefofer/yosefofer04.pdf link]) – based upon Yehoshua Kimchi’s comments in [https://archive.org/details/YellinTanakh this Tanakh].

'''B. The Leningrad Codex''' (Hebrew כתב־יד לנינגרד, also כתי"ל or ל or L, Leningrad B 19a)
* '''Photographs of the manuscript''': photos of the Leningrad Codex (for download) can be found at the Internet Archive [https://archive.org/details/Leningrad_Codex_Color_Images here] in full color (but without the cover and Masorah pages). [https://archive.org/details/Leningrad_Codex/page/n8/mode/2up Here] and [https://archive.org/details/leningrad-codex-full here], and at [https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Category:Codex_Leningradensis Wikimedia], they are in black and white, including the cover and the pages of the Masoretic Text.
* '''Digital transcription of the manuscript’s photographs''': [https://tanach.us/ Westminster website], updated version on the [https://www.hcanat.us/Tanach.xml UXLC website]. At either site you can see and download the manuscript page for any selected passage by clicking ‘Go’ next to ‘LC facsimile’.

===Additional Manuscripts (Tiberian and Yemenite)===

Photographs of additional manuscripts were compared in difficult places (some accessible online and some in the university library). They were selected based on the classification and description of the manuscripts closed to the Aleppo Codex in the last chapter of Israel Yeivin, ''The Aleppo Codex: Its Vocalization and Accentuation'' (Chapter 56, pages 357-375).

#'''‭‮כתי”ש‬''' or MS S (Torah)‬: Codex Sassoon 507. Full photographs of the manuscript are at Wikisource and the [https://archive.org/details/439-damascus-pentateuch-torah-masoretic-manuscript-10th-century Internet Archive].
#'''כתי"ל1''' or MS L1 (Torah). This is MS '''L'''eh'''m'''ann ("ל<sup>מ</sup>" in Breuer), which was previously MS #14 in the Karaite synagogue of Cairo. It was written by Samuel ben Jacob, who is also the scribe and masorete of the Leningrad Codex (L).<ref>Rabbi Mordecai Breuer published the ''masorah magna'' in this manuscript and analyzed it; see ''The Masorah Magna to the Pentateuch'' (New York, 1992), 2. vols. (published by the [https://www.manfredlehmann.com/index.html Manfred and Anne Lehmann Foundation]). He also compared its ''ga`yot'' to those of the Leningrad Codex. In the summer of 1984, when when Rabbi Breuer studied the manuscript, it was already in the possession of Dr. Manfred Lehmann in New York. Since Lehmann's death in 1997 its location is unknown, nor is there a photographic version available to scholars. Regarding this see Yosef Ofer, "הערת מסורה עתיקה בדפי שטיח בכת"י ל-מ לשמואל בן יעקב", ''Leshonenu'' 80:1-2 (2018), pp. 29-52. Therefore, all citations of this manuscript regarding ''ga`yot'' are based on Breuer's testimony.</ref>
#'''‭‮כתי”ל2‬''' or MS L2 (Torah, [https://www.nli.org.il/he/manuscripts/NNL_ALEPH000062829/NLI#$FL46975410 link]‬): Leningrad manuscript Firkovich B17. Written by Shlomo ben Buya’a, the same scribe who wrote the letter-text of the Aleppo Codex.<ref>This manuscript was labeled ל<sup>1</sup> by Yeivin (נו.16, pp. 366-367.).</ref>
#'''כתי”ב''' or MS B (Torah, [https://archive.org/details/BL_Or4445_Torah link‬]): Manuscript Or 4445 in the British Museum. (We did not use this manuscript in practice, because its photograph was not yet accessible during the preparation of the project, nor was it necessary to determine the wording according to the considerations set out in the third chapter of the introduction. It is true that this manuscript is of great importance as evidence of the development of the text of the Torah (in its letters, vocalization, and accents), and Breuer used it in his research for his book on the Aleppo Codex. We have therefore seldom mentioned the text of the manuscript in the notes of the text, relying on what is quoted from it in the Ginsburg edition (where it is referred to as “A”) and in Breuer’s notes.
#'''‬כתי”ו''' or MS W (Torah‬, [https://collections.museumofthebible.org/artifacts/34293-the-washington-pentateuch?&tab=description link]): a typical Tiberian manuscript that went online with high-quality photography courtesy of the Museum of the Bible in Washington D.C. in August 2019. We did not actually use this manuscript to prepare this edition, because its photograph was not yet accessible at the time. But we intend use it to supplement the documentation of the text in the Torah regarding the vocalization and accents and metegs in some places that are difficult to decide. The manuscript is clearly legible and is well-preserved for the most part until the beginning of Parshat Nitzavim (in some places, missing material has been filled in with other manuscripts, as at the end of the Torah). That being the case, it provides additional evidence for most of the missing Torah of the Aleppo Codex, which begins with the end of Deuteronomy.
#'''כתי”ק''' or MS C (Nevi'im‬): Cairo Codex of the Prophets.
#'''כתי”ת‬''' or MS T (Ketuvim): Cambridge Add. 1753, or '''"ק<sub>מ</sub>"''' ‮‬according to Breuer. The ת stands ‭for ‮תימני‬ (''Teimani'', "Yemenite"). This‬ Yemenite manuscript, from the 14th or 15th century, was described by Yeivin in his article, “The Division into Sections in the Book of Psalms”, ''Textus'' VII (1969), p. 80. According to Yeivin it is a direct or nearly direct copy of the Aleppo Codex itself. In practice, this manuscript is very important in matters of spelling and parashah in the books of the Ketuvim in the Aleppo Codex. A facsimile of this manuscript in microfilm can be found at the [https://www.nli.org.il/he/at-your-service/reading-rooms#manuscripts Institute of Microfilmed Hebrew Manuscripts] at the National Library, where it is filed under F 17490 F 17508. Details [https://merhav.nli.org.il/primo-explore/search?query=any,contains,000139700,AND&tab=default_tab&search_scope=Local&vid=NLI&lang=iw_IL&offset=0 here].
#'''כתי”ב1‬''' or MS B1 (Ketuvim‬): British Library Or 2375 (ב1 in Breuer). For full information on the manuscript and the additional volumes accompanying it (the books of the Nevi'im) see [https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Category:Prophets_and_Writings_with_Targum-Tafsir_BL_Or_2210-2211-2375 here].
#'''כתי”ש1‬''' or MS S1 (Bible‬): Manuscript Sassoon 1053 (ש1 in Yeivin and שׂ in Breuer). A photocopy of this manuscript in microfilm can be found at the [https://www.nli.org.il/he/at-your-service/reading-rooms#manuscripts Institute of Microfilmed Hebrew Manuscripts] at the National Library (filed under F 8881), with its details in the catalogue [https://merhav.nli.org.il/primo-explore/search?query=any,contains,000134958,AND&tab=default_tab&search_scope=Local&vid=NLI&lang=iw_IL&offset=0 here]. A full photocopy is available online (at the bottom of the page). Although the hundreds of photographs there are inefficient to use, we have uploaded them [https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Category:Tanakh-MS-Sassoon-1053 here] in useful PDF files, one file per book.

See also the [http://www.bl.uk/manuscripts/FullDisplay.aspx?ref=Or_9879 First Gaster Bible] (Egypt, 10th century), British Library Or 9879; and the [http://www.bl.uk/manuscripts/FullDisplay.aspx?ref=Or_9880 Second] (Egypt, 11th or 12th centuries), British Library Or 9880.

===Biblical Editions Based on Tiberian Manuscripts===

In places where the wording is unclear in manuscript photographs, we consulted manuscript-based editions to see how the editors deciphered the text. We referred to them by the following abbreviations:

====Editions based on the Leningrad Codex====

# '''BHS''' = תורה נביאים וכתובים ''Biblia Hebraica Stuttgartensia'' 1984.
# '''Dotan''' = the editions of Aron Dotan (Adi Publishing 2005/6; ''Biblia Hebraica Leningradensia'' 2001).
# '''הקלדה''' or '''המקליד''' = the‬ [https://tanach.us/ Westminster transcription] in its time, now [https://www.hcanat.us/Tanach.xml UXLC]. This is a digital transcription with textual comments under professional supervision. The free license in the first link is expected to change, but the same project continues further under the free license in the second link, and has important corrections and updates. This transcript was originally based on BHS (and not the Leningrad Codex directly), so it has errors originating from BHS and references to it in the comments.

====Editions based on the Aleppo Codex====

# '''Breuer''' = Editions of Rabbi Mordechai Breuer (Rabbi Kook Institute 5749; ''Horev'' 5757; ''Jerusalem Crown'' 5760).
# '''Mechon Mamre''' = the online edition of Mechon Mamre (based on the editions of Breuer).
# '''מג"ה''' = ''Mikra'ot Gedolot 'Haketer''',‬ edited by Menachem Cohen. We have used volumes published by Bar-Ilan University since the year 5752 (only since 2017 or 2018 has there been easy access to the [https://www.mgketer.org/mikra/1/1 online edition of the entire Bible]).
# '''''Simanim''''' = the '''Simanim''' Tanakh (Feldheim 5768).

===Printed Editions (Not Based on Tiberian Manuscripts)===

In many places we have documented the wording in common editions of the Tanakh, and particularly in places where the text of the original manuscript (the Aleppo or Leningrad Codex) raises a particular difficulty. '''However, these editions were rarely a consideration in determining the text itself.''' Rather, mentioning them them in textual notes is meant to document the development of the text in recent generations, as it is found in the most well-known and widely-read editions, and consequently to prevent surprises for people who compare those editions to ours.

# '''‮מ״ג'''‬ = ''Miqra'ot‬ Gedolot'', printed in Venice, Second Edition (c. 1523-25). We did not usually mention it in the text documentation, except in particular places where it was needed; instead, we noted in general the typically accepted wording found in the many printings that followed it (see #2 below). For a full scan, see the Internet Archive: [https://archive.org/details/The_Second_Rabbinic_Bible_Vol_1 Volume I] (Torah), [https://archive.org/details/The_Second_Rabbinic_Bible_Vol_2 Volume II] (Nevi'im Rishonim), [https://archive.org/details/The_Second_Rabbinic_Bible_Vol_3 Volume III] (Nevi'im Aḥaronim), [https://archive.org/details/The_Second_Rabbinic_Bible_Vol_4 Volume IV] (Ketuvim).<ref>For the first edition of the ''Miqra'ot Gedolot'' (Venice, 1516/7) see [https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH001725628/NLI here]. The first two editions were published by Daniel Bomberg in Venice. On the differences between them, see [https://seforimblog.com/2008/03/some-notes-regarding-first-second/ this article] (in English) by Jordan S. Penkower, which summarizes the findings of his doctoral dissertation.</ref>
# '''Printed editions (דפוסים)''' = selected [https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Category:Hebrew_bible_editions editions of the Hebrew Bible] from the 19th and early 20th centuries, some of them reprinted later in numerous fascimile editions. Among them: the editions of Heidenheim (Torah),<ref>Heidenheim printed the Torah several times. Except for the first time (''[https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH001268267/NLI Torat Elohim]'', which was published in the year 5557, and contained only the Book of Genesis to the middle of Parashat Mikeitz), the following times he reprinted the Pentateuch from exactly the same typset edition of the verses, but each time with different commentaries or translation. These volumes were published simultaneously by Heidenheim in the years 5578-5581: (1) the ''[https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH002478873/NLI Ḥumash Ma'or Einayim]'' (with the commentary ''`Ein ha-Kore'' for readers of the Torah, and the commentary ''`Ein ha-Sofer'' for scribes), (2) the ''[https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH001323914/NLI `Ein ha-Sofer]'' (with only the commentary ''`Ein ha-Sofer'' for scribes), (3) ''[https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH002478873/NLI Ḥumash Moda` la-Binah]'' (with Rashi's commentary and the commentary ''Havanat ha-Miqra'' by Heidenheim),  (4) ''[https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH001875998/NLI Sefer Torat Moshe]'' (with a German translation in Hebrew letters and the commentary ''Minḥah Ḥadashah''). Heidenheim's Torah was reprinted after his death in the [https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH002511577/NLI ''Ḥumash Rodelheim'' of 5607] [1846-7] (reprinted in 5624 [1863-4] [https://archive.org/details/hamishahhumsheto00unse/page/n4/mode/2up in Hebrew] alongside [https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH002094874/NLI a German translation]), and again in the [https://archive.org/details/Torah-with-Targum-and-Rashi_Rodelheim_1860/page/n6/mode/2up ''Ḥumash Rodelheim'' 5620] [1859-60], which has a revised text of Targum Onkelos and Rashi's commentary (Heidenheim had intended to publish a Pentateuch with Targum and Rashi during his lifetime, and in the ''Ḥumash Rodelheim'' of 5620, notes were printed which he had left in his own handwriting).</ref> Leeser-Jaquett (Miqra),<ref>[[:W:Isaac Leeser|Isaac Leeser]]'s bilingual Hebrew-English Pentateuch, ''The Law of God'', was published in a clear, beautiful edition (Philadelphia, 5605 [1845]): [https://archive.org/details/torathaelohim01lees/page/n8/mode/2up Genesis], [https://archive.org/details/torathaelohim02lees/page/n8/mode/2up Exodus], [https://archive.org/details/torathaelohim03lees/page/n8/mode/2up Leviticus], [https://archive.org/details/torathaelohim04lees/page/n8/mode/2up Numbers], [https://archive.org/details/torathaelohim05lees/page/n8/mode/2up Deuteronomy]. In his introduction to the edition (vol. 1 Genesis, p. vi) Leeser wrote that he did further proofreading of Heidenheim's edition; the content of the Hebrew pages in his Pentateuch matches that of Heidenheim's ''`Ein ha-Sofer''. Leeser's full Hebrew Bible was published in cooperation with Joseph Jaquett in Philadelphia, 1848. Reprints can be found [https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH004051225/NLI here] and [https://archive.org/details/torahneviimukhet0000unse/page/n9/mode/2up here] (both from the year 1868), and [https://archive.org/details/Tanakh_Leeser-Jaquett_1878/page/n8/mode/2up here] (1878). This edition by Leeser was the first time that a full Tanakh with vocalization was published in the New World.</ref> Letteris (Miqra),<ref>[[:W:Max Letteris|Meir Halevi Letteris]] published two editions of the Bible: the first one in 1852, in which there are two columns on each page and the letters are relatively small; the second in 1866, in which each page is a single column and the letters are clear and beautiful. Both editions were subsequently republished by the original publisher and in facsimile editions by other publishers, but it was the highly aesthetic second edition that received massive diffusion. A good scan of the first edition is '''[https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH002350725/NLI here]''' (1927 reprint), and an excellent scan of the second edition is '''[https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH002350725/NLI here]''' (1882 reprint). For scans of numerous reprints, some of them accompanied by translations, see '''[[:Commons:Category:Letteris bibles|Wikimedia Commons]]'''.</ref> Bamberger-Hildesheimer-Lehmann (Miqra),<ref>Abbreviated בה"ל. For a scan of good quality see '''[https://archive.org/details/Tanakh_Bamberger-Hildesheimer-Lehmann_Leipzig_1870/page/n4/mode/2up here]'''.</ref> Baer (Genesis, Nevi'im and Ketuvim),<ref>[https://archive.org/details/textummasoreticu01baer/page/n105/mode/2up Genesis] (Baer did not publish the rest of the Torah), [https://archive.org/details/librijosuaeetjud00baer/page/n6/mode/2up Joshua-Judges], [https://archive.org/details/textummasoreticu03baer/page/n163/mode/2up Samuel], [https://archive.org/details/liberregu00lips/page/n4/mode/2up Kings], [https://archive.org/details/textummasoreticu04baer/page/n105/mode/2up Isaiah], [https://archive.org/details/liberjeremiae00baer/page/n6/mode/2up Jeremiah], [https://archive.org/details/liberezechieli00baer/page/5/mode/2up Ezekiel], [https://archive.org/details/textummasoreticu07baer/page/n115/mode/2up The Twelve], [https://archive.org/details/liberpsalmorum00baer/page/n4/mode/2up Psalms], [https://archive.org/details/liberproverbior00baer/page/n6/mode/2up Proverbs], [https://archive.org/details/liberiobi00baer/page/n6/mode/2up Job], the [https://archive.org/details/quinquevolumina00baer/page/n4/mode/2up Five Megillot], [https://archive.org/details/textummasoreticu12baer/page/n201/mode/2up Daniel-Ezra-Nehemiah], [https://archive.org/details/liberchronicorum00baer/page/n6/mode/2up Chronicles]; [https://archive.org/search.php?query=Seligmann%20Baer%20AND%20mediatype%3Atexts additional scans of all volumes].</ref> Ginsburg (Miqra),<ref>[https://archive.org/details/massoreticocriti00gins First edition] (1894), and a second edition in four volumes: [https://archive.org/details/pentateuchu00gins/page/n4/mode/2up Torah] (1908), [https://archive.org/details/prophetaepriores00gins Nevi'im Rishonim] (1911), [https://archive.org/details/prophetaeposteri00gins Nevi'im Aḥaronim] (1911); a volume of Ketuvim was printed in the year 1926, and it will enter the public domain on 1 January 2022 (although [https://archive.org/details/liberpsalmorum00gins Psalms] was previously published separately in 1913). ''Torah Nevi'im and Ketuvim: The Jerusalem Edition'' edited by Umberto Cassuto (Jerusalem: Magnes, 5713) is based upon a facsimile of the Ginsburg's second edition.</ref> and the ''Miqra'ot Gedolot'' [https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Category:MG1859-Vienna-Torah Netter Edition] (Torah) and [https://he.wikisource.org/wiki/משתמש:Dovi/מקרא_על_פי_המסורה/מידע_על_מהדורה_זו/נספחים#cite_ref-13 Warsaw Edition] (Miqra). It is important to note that by "Printed editions" (דפוסים) we do ''not'' mean the Venice, 1525 edition of the ''Miqra'ot Gedolot'', which we denote instead by מ”ג (see #1 above). This is in contrast to how ד is used in Breuer.
# '''Koren (קורן)''' = the Koren Edition of the Tanakh (Jerusalem, 5722).

===Additional Tools===

In addition to photographs of the manuscripts and printings of the Tanakh, we also used the following tools:

# The comments of the editor in the [https://tanach.us/ Westminster transcript];
# The broad commentary on the Masoretic notes found in the Leningrad Codex found in BHQ (in the volumes which have already appeared, and especially in the volumes on the Five Scrolls and Ezra-Nehemiah);<ref>We used BHQ mainly for the places where the text of the Leningrad Codex does not match its Masoretic comments, and also for extensive information that exists in it regarding the divisions of the parashot in the manuscripts.</ref>
# Aron Dotan's lists at the end of the volumes in his two editions ("Adi" and _BHL_); 
# The books and articles of Israel Yeivin, particularly his masterpiece – ''The Aleppo Codex: Its Vocalization and Accentuation'';<ref>(Jerusalem: Magnes, 5729). Any general reference to ‘Yeivin’ is to this book.</ref>
# The books and articles and lists<ref>“Lists” refers to the list “The Text and its Sources” printed at the beginning of the volumes in the Da`at Miqra series (Rabbi Kook Institute), and to the lists concerning the wording at the end of the three editions of the Bible.</ref> of Rabbi Mordechai Breuer (especially ''The Aleppo Codex and the Received Text of the Bible''. <ref>Jerusalem: Rabbi Kook Institute, 5737.</ref> Any general reference to “Breuer” is a reference to this book.).
# The elaborate introduction to the text of the Bible in ''Mikra'ot Gedolot 'Haketer''' (in the volume Joshua-Judges, 5752).
# [https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Category:Masoretic_works The classical Masoretic literature], especially ''Minḥat Shai''.

===Divisions of the Parashot===

As for the divisions of the parashot into open sections (''petuḥot'') and closed sections (''setumot''), we used the information and sources presented in [https://he.wikipedia.org/wiki/en:Parashah this article].

